@page "/"

<PageTitle>dotcc: compile C &amp; Zig to .NET and WebAssembly, in your browser</PageTitle>

<section class="hero">
    <h1>Compile <span class="hl-c">C</span> and <span class="hl-zig">Zig</span> to <span class="hl-net">.NET&nbsp;/&nbsp;C#</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">
        <strong>dotcc</strong> is a clang-shaped compiler front-end that transpiles C and Zig
        to AOT-clean C&nbsp;14 on .NET&nbsp;10, with a second back-end for WebAssembly text.
        Two languages, one typed IR, two back-ends. It compiles real programs whole, and
        <a href="python">CPython 3.13</a> is one of them. This page runs the <em>real</em>
        compiler, itself compiled to wasm, entirely in your browser tab.
    </p>
    <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="cta primary" href="sandbox">Open the sandbox →</a>
        <a class="cta" href="coverage">Browse coverage</a>
        <a class="cta" href="python">CPython on .NET</a>
        <a class="cta ghost" href="https://github.com/sebgod/dotcc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source on GitHub ↗</a>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="arch">
    <h2>N front-ends × M back-ends, meeting at one typed IR</h2>
    <div class="nm">
        <div class="nm-col">
            <span class="nm-label">front-ends</span>
            <div class="nm-node c">C</div>
            <div class="nm-node zig">Zig</div>
        </div>
        <div class="nm-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
        <div class="nm-core">
            typed IR
            <small>a <code>CType</code> on every node</small>
        </div>
        <div class="nm-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
        <div class="nm-col">
            <span class="nm-label">back-ends</span>
            <div class="nm-node net">C# / .NET</div>
            <div class="nm-node wat">WebAssembly</div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="arch-note">
        Each front-end lexes and parses its own LALR(1) grammar, then lowers into the
        <em>same</em> IR, and every back-end realizes that one IR. There is no pairwise
        special-casing: today's 2×2 is C and Zig to C# and wat. Adding a language is a new
        front-end, and adding a target is a new back-end. They meet in the middle.
    </p>
</section>

