<section class="pipe">
    <h2>How a run works</h2>
    <p class="pipe-intro">
        Nothing leaves your browser. The C# back-end targets the CLR (a normal .NET program);
        the <strong>wat</strong> back-end is what makes execution possible here, because a
        browser tab can run WebAssembly without a server. The sandbox uses it end to end:
    </p>
    <ol class="pipe-steps">
        <li><span class="step-n">1</span> Your source is staged in an in-browser (MEMFS) filesystem.</li>
        <li><span class="step-n">2</span> dotcc, running as wasm, parses it and lowers it to the typed IR.</li>
        <li><span class="step-n">3</span> The <code>wat</code> back-end emits WebAssembly text.</li>
        <li><span class="step-n">4</span> <code>wabt.js</code> assembles that text to a wasm module.</li>
        <li><span class="step-n">5</span> A small WASI layer runs the program (its <code>_start</code>, then <code>main</code>), capturing stdout, stderr and the exit code.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="pipe-note">
        The <strong>C#</strong> and <strong>-E</strong> tabs in the sandbox show the same compile
        projected the other ways: the view inside the compiler.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="cards credits">
    <h2>Built on LALR.CC, by SharpAstro</h2>
    <p class="arch-note">
        Both front-ends run on <a href="https://github.com/SharpAstro/LALR.CC" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LALR.CC</a>,
        an LALR(1) parser-table generator and runtime for .NET: it reads a grammar written in
        YAML and generates, at build time, the parse tables and the typed syntax tree and
        visitor that dotcc's C and Zig binders are written against. LALR.CC comes from
        <a href="https://sharpastro.github.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SharpAstro</a>, a .NET ecosystem for
        astrophotography, as does the other work below.
    </p>

